mathematical-proxy-4e628999.symmetry_origin:
This formula originates from the principle of superposition for electric potentials, which states that the total potential at a point due to a collection of charges is the algebraic sum of the potentials due to individual charges. Each term itself is derived from the potential of a single point charge, 
V = \frac{1}{4\pi\varepsilon_0} \frac{Q}{r}
, which arises from Gauss's Law and the definition of potential as the negative line integral of the electric field. The specific arrangement of charges along the z-axis gives the system azimuthal (rotational) symmetry around the z-axis.

tight-binding-energy-dispersion-relation.limits_and_boundary:
In the limit of no hopping (i.e., all 
\gamma(\mathbf{R}) = 0
), the energy becomes a constant 
E(\mathbf{k}) = \epsilon_0 - \beta
, representing perfectly localized atomic-like states with no band formation. This corresponds to the case of isolated atoms or a very dilute gas. When the wavevector 
\mathbf{k}
 approaches zero (long wavelength limit, 
\mathbf{k} \to 0
), the exponential term 
e^{i\mathbf{k} \cdot \mathbf{R}}
 approaches 1, and the dispersion relation simplifies to 
E(0) = \epsilon_0 - \beta - \sum_{\mathbf{R}} \gamma(\mathbf{R})
. This represents the energy at the center of the Brillouin zone. As 
\mathbf{k}
 varies from the center to the edge of the Brillouin zone, the exponential term changes sign or magnitude, leading to the characteristic energy bands. At the Brillouin zone boundaries, specific values of 
\mathbf{k}
 lead to standing wave-like solutions and often define the extrema of the energy bands (band edges). In the limit of very weak interatomic coupling (small 
\gamma(\mathbf{R})
), the energy bands will be very narrow. Conversely, strong coupling leads to wider bands.

E(\\mathbf{k}).description:
The energy of an electron in the crystal lattice, dependent on its wavevector 
\mathbf{k}
. This is the energy eigenvalue of the electronic state.

\\gamma(\\mathbf{R}).description:
A parameter that quantifies the energy associated with an electron hopping or tunneling between an atomic site and another site located at a lattice vector 
\mathbf{R}
 away. It is typically non-zero only for nearest and next-nearest neighbor sites, rapidly decaying with distance.
